WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.310
DAN MCCLARY: This year
in Cloud SQL we've really

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.040
focused on three large areas--

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.620
overall performance and scale--
make all of our databases

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.650
faster, make them scale more
easily, make as much of it

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.160
one touch as possible; overall
reliability and availability,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.960
because if your database isn't
available, it isn't reliable,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.585
what good is it?

00:00:24.585 --> 00:00:25.960
And then really
full integration,

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.410
trying to integrate it more
deeply within GCP itself,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.170
and then also with
the things out

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.670
in the ecosystems of MySQL and
Postgres that you care about,

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.082
and again, things like
SQL Server now as well.

00:00:35.082 --> 00:00:37.540
And we're proud of every single
thing we've done this year.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.560
But I think a few that
really warrant discussion are

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.390
things like Postgres going GA.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.500
9.6 is GA.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.590
And again, 11 is in beta.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.260
Our private IP work.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.680
And then our regional expansion
we'll talk a little bit

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.070
about as well.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.712
So lots and lots
of stuff this year.

00:00:52.712 --> 00:00:53.670
Lots and lots of stuff.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.483
Lots of new regions.

00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:57.150
One of the things
people may have missed

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.580
is many, many more flags.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.780
We have for a long time had
a pretty restrictive policy

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.180
on what flags we
allowed administrators

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.280
to set for the
database instances.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.847
And ultimately, we've kind
of decided that in many cases

00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:11.430
you know best.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.450
You know what it is
you're trying to do.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.750
And so if there was a
flag in MySQL or Postgres

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.780
you were looking for, and you
couldn't find it, check again.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.662
Both in our documentation
and in our console UI,

00:01:21.662 --> 00:01:23.370
you'll find most of
the flags that you're

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.120
looking for for Postgres
and MySQL are now there.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.230
We've upgraded our proxy.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:32.255
We've added pushy IS
supports for JSON C. My SQL

00:01:32.255 --> 00:01:33.630
external master
has been a really

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.570
great way for organizations to
start to migrate their MySQL

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.960
workloads into a managed
system with Cloud SQL.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.970
Rotating SSL certificates.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.440
Real, real interesting
move on the security side.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.190
And then connections
from Cloud Functions,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.857
which I think over
time is going to grow

00:01:48.857 --> 00:01:54.020
into a richer kind of external
and trigger system as well.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.520
So again, highlight:
Postgres GA.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.910
We're seeing really,
really great adoption

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:00.520
in the Postgres side
of the offering.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.820
I think those of you who know
about the growth of Postgres

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.800
over the last several
years in the community

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.960
understand that for certain
enterprise functions

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.372
it really is becoming the open
source database of choice.

00:02:10.372 --> 00:02:11.830
It's part of the
reason we're going

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.163
to have Simon join us to talk
through some of the things

00:02:14.163 --> 00:02:16.630
we might want to know
as we move into 11.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:20.170
But with Postgres going GA, that
means we now have SLA coverage.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.020
And it's going to be a major
focus for us in the year

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.070
to come as well.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:26.850
High availability.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.920
Again, if your
database isn't there,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.400
It's not really worth it.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.400
So high availability has
been a big focus for us

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.010
on both the Postgres and
MySQL sides of the house.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.170
And it's worth breaking
these out a little bit,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.110
because we're doing them
a little bit differently.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.100
Now on the MySQL
side of the house,

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.710
you can add high availability
with just a single click.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.710
And what you're getting
is bin log replications.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.660
You're getting
semi-synchronous replication,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.380
and we're monitoring replication
lag as our failover signal.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.600
Now that means that there's
no app level difference

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.840
for highly available stuff.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.648
It's the same as a
single interface.

00:02:57.648 --> 00:02:59.190
But you do have the
option to use it.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.690
And that secondary replica is
a little bit of a read replica

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:02.472
as well.

00:03:02.472 --> 00:03:04.680
But you're dealing with the
notion of replication lag

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:05.520
as well.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.440
We're doing things a
little bit differently

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:08.340
on the Postgres side.

00:03:12.003 --> 00:03:14.670
And this is actually going to be
the strategy more broadly going

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.575
forward for our high
availability for SQL Server,

00:03:17.575 --> 00:03:19.200
for Postgres and
maybe we'll eventually

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.250
bring it back on
MySQL world as well,

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.050
which is actually to build on
replicated persistent disks.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.820
Now this is a truly
synchronous replication.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.210
And so we're actually
replicating the block device

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:31.900
underneath the instance.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.300
And to be honest,
this is a thing

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.040
we're really only able
to do because of Google's

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.180
overall network capacity.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.930
It's very, very demanding
to replicate a whole block

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.117
device synchronously.

00:03:42.117 --> 00:03:43.950
And what this means,
though, is that there's

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.240
no replication lag.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.410
And it changes our failover
metric a little bit.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.220
So in this case, if we get
60 seconds with no heartbeat,

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.650
we know we need to failover.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.590
Now you can't use that
instance as a read replica.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:59.610
But you can set up regular
read replicas to use otherwise.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.140
And you can continue
to serve data

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.230
from those from a healthy
zone, even if that primary has

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:07.610
gone down and we're in the
process of failing over.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.630
And then private IP,
I think, is actually

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.330
a big deal for the
security [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.080
and for those who are
latency sensitive as

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.170
well private IP is now available
throughout the Cloud Sequel

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:16.920
line.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.649
And this is going
to allow you to peer

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:23.490
your VPC with the Google VPC
in which our instance lives.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:25.030
Now what does that mean for you?

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.880
Well, that means lower
latency, for one.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.310
It also means that all
traffic is encrypted.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.070
You can apply TLS to that
as well if you want to.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.200
But it's going to be
encrypted and never exposed

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.390
to the public internet.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:38.370
And that is going
to be a major driver

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.400
for certain organizations that
are network security sensitive.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:46.970
Wrong green button.

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.137
And then regional expansion.

00:04:48.137 --> 00:04:49.953
I think this has been
a highlight for GCP

00:04:49.953 --> 00:04:51.620
for the last three
years running now, is

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.530
just how rapidly we're trying
to expand across the globe.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.053
And we're now in 58 zones.

00:04:57.053 --> 00:04:58.970
I'm not sure exactly how
many regions that is.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:03.270
It's many regions, each
with at least three zones.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.600
And this is really giving
you the opportunity

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.572
to locate your databases
closest to where

00:05:08.572 --> 00:05:10.280
you need to run your
application, closest

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.470
to where you need
to do business.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.390
And again, this
allows us the ability

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:17.900
to have our replication exist
in any one of the regions

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:22.410
with, again, good failure
domains across the zones.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.940
And now let's get on to the
next part of our discussion.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:26.790
We're going to dig more into the
meat of some of the offerings

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:27.290
themselves.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:28.980
We're going to talk
about PostgreSQL 11,

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.480
and a lot of things
you should know.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.820
If you've only been running
9.6 or maybe running 10,

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.680
there are a lot of
improvements in 11

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.750
that are really
interesting to talk about.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.980
And we're really, really lucky
to have Simon Riggs with us.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.610
Simon, if you want to come up.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.040
Simon is the founder
and CEO of 2ndQuadrant.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:47.530
2ndQuadrant provides a wide
variety of Postgres services

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:48.358
in over-- what--

00:05:48.358 --> 00:05:48.900
20 countries?

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:50.820
Is that right?

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:54.000
And collectively they have
over 200 personal years

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.900
of Postgres experience.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.500
Now importantly, Simon is not
just the CEO of 2ndQuadrant.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.700
He's also an active developer
and committer on Postgres.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.730
And he's helped bring things
like PITR to the community

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.708
and partitioning
the replication.

00:06:06.708 --> 00:06:08.250
So we're going to
get the opportunity

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.000
not only to see Postgres
work in the field

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.550
and talk about
that, but also then

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.008
sort of the
committer's perspective

00:06:13.008 --> 00:06:14.710
on where the database is going.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.526
So please welcome Simon.

00:06:17.526 --> 00:06:20.233
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:20.233 --> 00:06:21.400
Simon, thank you for coming.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.358
SIMON RIGGS: All right.

00:06:22.358 --> 00:06:23.090
No problem.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.250
DAN MCCLARY: This is going
to be a little awkward.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:25.000
SIMON RIGGS: Yeah.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.142
Don't worry.

00:06:26.142 --> 00:06:27.100
DAN MCCLARY: All right.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.630
So let's start to talk
first about parallelisms.

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:31.780
I think one of the
things I notice in 11

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:35.293
is there's been a lot of
enhancements to parallelism.

00:06:35.293 --> 00:06:36.460
We see it in parallel query.

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:39.440
We see it in
parallel index build.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.080
So one thing I wonder
about is, given

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.290
that scan types and joint types
are now increasingly parallel,

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.540
what are some things
that users should

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.580
know when they think about
how parallelism is going

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.480
to apply in their database?

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.170
SIMON RIGGS: OK.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:58.440
Well, I think in
9.6 we introduced

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.060
the basics of parallelism.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.550
But we didn't
really roll that out

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:05.700
to everything that
could be parallelized.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.640
Whereas by the time
we get to Postgres 11,

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:10.890
the journey's almost done.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.220
If you look at what
we've done in Postgres 12

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:15.600
release cycle,
mostly bug fixing.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.250
And I'm actually very happy that
we got the main join types done

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.890
in Postgres 10 and 11.

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:27.000
So for example, initially
we did hash joins.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:31.740
But they didn't use
a single hash table,

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.590
though they had a
separate hash table per--

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:34.590
DAN MCCLARY: [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.830
SIMON RIGGS: Yeah,
which was very wasteful.

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:40.980
So really we're at
the stage of hitting

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:44.797
the secondary and tertiary
issues with parallelism.

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:45.630
So it's almost done.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.420
We're very happy with it.

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:49.500
DAN MCCLARY: And so if
users haven't really

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.440
thought about how parallelism--
how to take advantage

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:54.120
of parallel query, are there
things that they should think

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:55.870
about in terms of
setting up their schema,

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:58.530
the way they think about
table architecture,

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:01.260
that will help
increase their ability

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:04.322
to leverage parallelism?

00:08:04.322 --> 00:08:05.030
SIMON RIGGS: Yes.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:11.330
I mean, it's possible to get
large parallel databases that

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:14.190
exist out there with
different databases.

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.520
But with Postgres
what we've focused on

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:19.320
is small parallelism.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.270
So intranode parallelism.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.050
So really we're
looking for queries

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:28.310
that can be somewhere between
two and four times faster

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:31.290
using this intranode
parallelism.

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:35.210
So really a lot
of important areas

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:39.559
occur in applications, where
we've got certain queries that

00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:41.720
are a bit slower
than they should be,

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.770
but they're not running
for necessarily hours.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:47.250
They could be like things
that run for, say, a minute.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.990
And if we can get that down
to 10 seconds or two seconds,

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.640
then it's goal achieved.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:55.280
So we're not really
talking about

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.300
the same world-encompassing
parallelism

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.080
that you get from
some other products.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:08.180
But making simple changes to
the response time of real world

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:09.540
queries.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:10.290
DAN MCCLARY: Yeah.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.600
It seems that we would
look to see that maybe

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.570
in operational
workloads that are

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.340
dependent on real low latency
or operational reporting

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:19.380
and things like that?

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.070
SIMON RIGGS: Yes.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:23.050
I think what we're
seeing with Postgres

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:26.790
is that the old idea
of an OLTP system where

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:33.400
all the transactions are short
isn't really what we see now.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:36.340
There's a whole mixed
workload where you get OLTP,

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:40.580
but also complex analytical
queries happening immediately.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.670
For example, asking
the question,

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.410
where's your
nearest coffee shop?

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:47.980
Or what's the best hotel
for this particular customer

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:49.750
to stay in?

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:52.630
They need to be
quick, but they're not

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:57.120
short in terms of the analytical
power required to answer them.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.692
And those are the things
that we're targeting now.

00:10:00.692 --> 00:10:02.400
DAN MCCLARY: I suppose
as the modern OLTP

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.803
database becomes more
analytically oriented,

00:10:05.803 --> 00:10:06.720
this actually matters.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.720
This notion of going from
a minute to 10 seconds

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:09.882
could be huge.

00:10:09.882 --> 00:10:11.340
So another thing
that's interesting

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.920
is parallelism in index builds.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.450
And I know people
in the MySQL world

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.533
would be familiar with
things like parallel apply.

00:10:17.533 --> 00:10:18.241
SIMON RIGGS: Yes.

00:10:18.241 --> 00:10:19.783
DAN MCCLARY: Can
you say a little bit

00:10:19.783 --> 00:10:21.500
about how parallelism
for background work

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:22.500
is evolving in Postgres?

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:31.070
SIMON RIGGS: So in terms of
maintenance functions, I mean,

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.710
we've got parallel vacuum.

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:38.390
We've got parallel index build,
which the main ones, really.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:42.200
We have moved away
from the idea of trying

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:43.910
to do maintenance on indexes.

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:49.100
So just rebuilding the
index is a practical task.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.710
And because we can
do that concurrently,

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.750
it's fairly simple
to build a new index

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:57.470
rather than try to
maintain the other one.

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:00.230
There aren't a whole
load of other operations

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:02.520
that you'd need to
do in the background.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:05.990
But one thing we have done
is parallelized Create Table

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:09.860
as Select, which is quite
important for building summary

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:13.610
tables and such like, which
is an important application

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:14.540
technique.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:18.820
So that's really one of the key
things, I think, was the CTAS.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:19.570
DAN MCCLARY: Yeah.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:21.180
CTAS improvement is
actually really huge.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:23.180
I mean, you're talking
about any kind of summary

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:26.450
either pre-offload to warehouse
or for operational reporting.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:29.433
That can have a huge impact.

00:11:29.433 --> 00:11:31.350
So let's talk a little
bit about partitioning.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:33.600
So I look at 11, and I think,
this is the partitioning

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:34.660
release in a lot of ways.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:37.120
And you were very
early in partitioning.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:39.580
But one of the things that
people may not know about 11

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.123
versus previous versions is
that we're no longer strictly

00:11:42.123 --> 00:11:43.540
in sort of table
inheritance mode.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.360
We've got table
attribute partitioning.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:47.050
It's native.

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:48.682
But if you're
coming from Postgres

00:11:48.682 --> 00:11:50.140
and you've thought
about, oh, well.

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:51.473
You know, I read Stack Overflow.

00:11:51.473 --> 00:11:53.170
And I saw these
inherited tables.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:54.090
It's different now.

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.710
And so what should
implementers think about either

00:11:56.710 --> 00:12:00.250
as they're moving from
9.6 upwards or 10 upwards,

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.125
or if they're designing
a schema from scratch

00:12:02.125 --> 00:12:03.542
and trying to think
about how do I

00:12:03.542 --> 00:12:06.010
take the best advantage of
the variety of partitioning

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:07.990
options?

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:08.890
SIMON RIGGS: So yeah.

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:11.730
I mean, I was originally
involved in the work

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:14.270
on partitioning
back 10 years ago.

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:17.170
And frankly, if I'd
done-- not to dis myself,

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:19.930
but the work that we
did there was crude.

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:23.410
And it led to some
shortcomings, really,

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:26.740
with the types of application
that we could support.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.110
So what we've done now
is completely redesign

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:33.470
that, implement it from scratch
the way it should be done.

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:35.260
And as a result,
we're able to support

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:38.530
things like unique
indexes on partition

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:40.830
tables and foreign keys.

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:44.510
Obviously very key
for applications.

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:46.780
And it's really made a
big difference to the way

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.030
that people are able
to use this now.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:52.600
So partitioning isn't even
done yet, really, in 11.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:55.750
We've still got a lot of good
performance features coming

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:58.700
in 12 and in 13.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:03.130
So partitioning is a
really big application area

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:06.790
for Postgres and maybe
other databases as well.

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:09.250
But we see those requirements
coming very strongly

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:12.190
from a whole range of
users, because there's

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:14.760
just so much data these days
that people want to log.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:18.765
And partitioning really
makes that effective.

00:13:18.765 --> 00:13:20.390
DAN MCCLARY: So I
guess just to poke on

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:23.240
that a little bit, given the
wide variety of things that

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:25.220
are now present in
partitioning, do

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:28.670
you think that opens the door
for organizations or users

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:32.330
who are looking to move off of
other RTP maps that have had

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:34.520
maybe expensive
partitioning packages

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:37.123
or something like that?

00:13:37.123 --> 00:13:39.290
I mean, do you think that
the level of functionality

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:41.457
is enough that there's a
whole new wave of workloads

00:13:41.457 --> 00:13:45.110
that can come to Progress
with 11's partitioning?

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:46.070
SIMON RIGGS: Yes.

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:50.570
I mean, the style of feature
development with Postgres

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:53.480
is because we're releasing
features annually,

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:56.360
we will release a feature
and then add things to our

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:59.510
over the next couple of years.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:02.780
Maybe five or 10 years
ago you'd wait five years

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:03.810
for the next release.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:06.440
And then it would
all come in one rush,

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:08.840
except there would still
be stuff broken, right?

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:11.550
And then you'd wait another
five years for it to be fixed.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:15.180
So the Postgres way is to
release stuff a lot earlier.

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:19.010
What we have with the
initial partitioning is

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:23.630
select queries will
be fully optimized.

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:26.270
Inserts are optimized into it.

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:28.280
It's not really
until Postgres 11

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:32.040
that updates and deletes are
beginning to be optimized.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.390
And we've done more work
on that in 12 as well.

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:39.030
So the range of
workloads is evolving.

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:42.470
So if you want to
do insert only, sort

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:46.730
of append only workloads, they
work just fine in Postgres 11.

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:49.100
But we'll be moving
on to a whole range

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:51.230
of other applications.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:54.620
Your slides there mentioned
partition wise joins

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:56.630
and partition movement.

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:58.430
Those two features
are particularly

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:02.000
relevant for sharding
style applications built

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.240
using partitioning, whether
the partitions actually

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.980
exist on separate nodes.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.950
And that's really a
direction that we're

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:13.550
moving in for the
next couple of pieces

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:15.060
is that sharding concept.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:19.280
So not too long away from
having a fully sharded database

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:21.437
running on Postgres.

00:15:21.437 --> 00:15:22.520
DAN MCCLARY: That's great.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:25.250
I guess you make an interesting
point there around the Postgres

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:26.325
release cadence.

00:15:26.325 --> 00:15:27.950
And I think for people
who are thinking

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:31.370
about planning potential
migrations to Postgres,

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:34.220
if partitioning is a strong
requirement, this notion

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.650
that we can begin to
plan our migration now

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.980
as the features are
set but improving,

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:42.840
it maybe can actually reduce
some of the risk profile

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:44.322
of that kind of a migration.

00:15:44.322 --> 00:15:45.530
SIMON RIGGS: Yes, definitely.

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:49.520
I think what some
people say is that they

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:53.510
won't move to a release until
it's been out for six months.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:56.750
Well, obviously with
Postgres coming out annually

00:15:56.750 --> 00:16:01.000
you don't really have to wait
as long as you did before.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:04.370
And quite frankly,
the profile of testing

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:06.830
has changed dramatically
in software generally,

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:09.500
and most specifically
in Postgres.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:13.400
So whereas we used to rely
on the kind of many eyeballs

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:15.920
effect for people
reporting queries,

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:20.942
the extent of our test
automation is now--

00:16:20.942 --> 00:16:22.400
I mean, I can't
put a number on it.

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:25.700
But it's much different
to how it was.

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:30.110
And so you have a lot of
trust in the .0 release

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:32.390
now, whereas before
people said, well,

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:35.240
maybe wait for the .1, .2.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:37.910
But you know, it's usable,
really, from the word go.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:39.980
So every September now
you should be thinking

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:41.230
about upgrading your Postgres.

00:16:43.555 --> 00:16:45.180
DAN MCCLARY: That's
really interesting.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:46.980
So let's talk about
another one of these, hey,

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:48.772
maybe I have an expensive
database at home,

00:16:48.772 --> 00:16:50.770
and I'm interested in
getting away from it.

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:54.420
Another huge thing in
11 is stored procedures.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:57.600
And I think the community has
had functions for a long time.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:01.620
But procedures, they play a real
role in ensuring conformance,

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:05.130
in reducing network
traffic, a bunch of things.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.380
And you guys at 2ndQuadrant
actually did a lot of work

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:09.609
on the stored
procedures as well.

00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:11.553
Can you talk a little
bit about what you think

00:17:11.553 --> 00:17:12.720
they mean for the community?

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:15.420
And I guess same question--

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:20.579
if I'm thinking about coming
from a database that is

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:24.150
on premises or is maybe
something I'm trying to get off

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:26.069
of, but stored procedures
have been a blocker,

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:28.111
how is the door open now
in a way that it wasn't?

00:17:30.232 --> 00:17:32.190
SIMON RIGGS: I mean,
there's a small difference

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:33.840
between procedures
and functions.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.030
I mean, functions are
essentially-- well,

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:38.330
procedures are--

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:38.910
you know.

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:41.110
They're very similar
in many ways.

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:42.930
But the key
difference that we've

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:45.120
introduced with
stored procedures

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:47.550
is the ability to
run transactions

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:49.350
inside the procedure.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:52.230
And that is a really
pivotal difference.

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:56.130
It's important for migration
from other databases.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:59.400
But it allows you to do a whole
range of things you really

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:01.030
weren't able to do before.

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:04.900
So batch style query processing.

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:07.800
And we're also moving towards
autonomous transactions

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:10.330
within procedures, perhaps
in the next release.

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:14.640
But the style of programming
changes completely,

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:17.190
because when you had a
function that would only

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:19.560
exist within one
transaction, you're

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:23.370
now having a procedure that
can exist perhaps generating

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:26.760
thousands of transactions
as it executes completely

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:29.280
different style of programming.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:31.950
And it really moves a
lot of the business logic

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:34.170
through to the server now.

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.480
Because whereas
before you had to ask

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:39.660
for one transaction and
then another transaction,

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:41.250
the procedure can
just do it all.

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:44.110
It's like, come back
when you've finished now.

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:46.620
And that means that the
network traffic that you're

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:49.310
reducing between the
application and the database

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:50.940
is really considerable now.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:54.360
So we get the full benefit
of offloading things

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.550
to the database.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:19:00.300
Obviously not every single
piece of your application

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:01.670
should live in the database.

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:02.250
OK?

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:03.960
I think some people
tried to persuade

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:05.670
people that was a good idea.

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:06.690
And it's not.

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:07.290
OK?

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:09.960
But there are some
things that make sense

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:11.600
to be close to the data.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:12.520
OK.

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:15.130
And for those, it's ideal.

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:17.130
DAN MCCLARY: It's interesting
that you mentioned

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:18.880
the notion of having
multiple transactions

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:20.010
within the procedure.

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:23.553
I recall working in financial
services and other places

00:19:23.553 --> 00:19:25.470
that the notion of
straight through processing

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.650
can be a real impediment
for certain organizations

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:31.728
that need to make sure that
when this row is touched,

00:19:31.728 --> 00:19:33.270
all of these other
roles are updated.

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:37.070
And so this really
opens the door to that.

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:39.270
Do you find that
you're seeing growth

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:41.970
in the sort of regulated
industries on Postgres?

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:43.050
SIMON RIGGS: Absolutely.

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:43.550
Yeah.

00:19:43.550 --> 00:19:47.850
I mean, probably half
of our customer base

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:50.940
is financial services now.

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:55.050
Very widely used across
financial industries.

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:58.930
And when I say that, I think
some people think, oh, right.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.450
So somewhere in
the back room he's

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:02.820
got like one Postgres
database, and he's

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:04.270
claiming that as usage.

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:04.770
Right?

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:05.270
No.

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:09.590
I'm talking about
card processing is now

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:11.670
frontline done on Postgres.

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:16.890
So if you buy something on
various cloud providers,

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:21.210
you will find that that goes
through Postgres databases.

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:24.630
And there's literally hundreds
of billions of dollars

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:29.610
annually processed directly on
Postgres databases supported

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:30.990
by us, by the way.

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:32.490
DAN MCCLARY: Well,
there's something

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:33.600
interesting within
that too when you

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:35.308
start to talk about
global organizations.

00:20:35.308 --> 00:20:38.415
And I know one of the
areas that 2ndQuadrant's

00:20:38.415 --> 00:20:41.040
done a lot of work beyond things
like stored procedures is also

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:45.420
in logical replication,
things like BDR.

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:47.760
Could you talk for a
moment about how you're

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:52.442
seeing sort of cross
regional replication emerging

00:20:52.442 --> 00:20:54.900
in the Postgres community and
the kinds of use cases you're

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:55.530
seeing for it?

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:59.040
Be it disaster recovery or
split transaction processing,

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:01.440
the sorts of things that
really show up there.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:02.700
SIMON RIGGS: Certainly.

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:06.630
Yeah, I think there's various
ways of doing high availability

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:08.460
within a single region.

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:10.410
But when you go
cross-region, things

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:12.330
become a lot more complex.

00:21:12.330 --> 00:21:16.380
And some of the storage
replication techniques

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:20.590
that are possible become
much more problematic.

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:23.070
So Postgres has
invested a lot of time,

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:27.720
and 2ndQuadrant as well, in what
we call logical replication.

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:30.540
And the purpose of
that is to filter out

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:34.470
all of the physical
replication stuff

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:37.110
so that we're just
down to the basic data.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.300
So that reduces
bandwidth, but it also

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:44.340
allows you to do things like
upgrade between release.

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:47.820
And it certainly makes
cross-region replication

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:49.740
significantly easier.

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:51.960
And with something
like BDR, we actually

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:55.950
have global geosharding
where you can actually

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:58.980
have pools of data in
different continents,

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.510
and yet all working together.

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:05.100
So again, important
technologies, especially

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:07.522
in regulated industries.

00:22:07.522 --> 00:22:08.980
DAN MCCLARY: And
just in case folks

00:22:08.980 --> 00:22:11.253
don't know what BDR is--
bi-directional replication.

00:22:11.253 --> 00:22:13.420
I think you and I know
enough, it's, like, oh, well.

00:22:13.420 --> 00:22:16.260
Of course this.

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:19.130
So we talked a little bit about
the importance of analytics

00:22:19.130 --> 00:22:20.240
within the Postgres world.

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:21.782
And I mean, in a
lot of ways Postgres

00:22:21.782 --> 00:22:25.730
is really well-positioned to be
both your analytical workhorse

00:22:25.730 --> 00:22:28.560
for operational things
and your OLTP database.

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:32.240
The other thing that really
stood out to me in 11

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:34.580
is the changes to
analytical SQL.

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:38.690
And I think in many ways I
look at PostgreSQL syntax

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:42.230
as being in a lot of cases
at the very edge of how we

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:44.510
think about the SQL language.

00:22:44.510 --> 00:22:45.980
So for folks who
have looked at it,

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:47.753
Postgres 11 gives
us frame exclusions,

00:22:47.753 --> 00:22:49.670
which I think are really,
really powerful ways

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:52.760
to actually remove things
from the windowing frame,

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:54.867
and then also group
frame units, which is--

00:22:54.867 --> 00:22:56.450
I don't think any
other database today

00:22:56.450 --> 00:22:58.790
does group frame windowing.

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:01.490
Can you talk a little
bit about each of these

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:05.100
and what it means to
the analytical workload?

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:05.850
SIMON RIGGS: Sure.

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:08.430
So, I think what a
lot of people don't

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:13.350
realize with SQL is that it's
had window functions in it

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:14.680
for some time now.

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:16.650
And what a window
function is it allows

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:20.160
you to look at data
in an ordered manner.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:23.730
And this is especially
important for time series, where

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.870
if you're thinking about
your data as a graph where

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:30.090
the points change
over time, then

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:33.810
you can write queries that
analyze that data so that you

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:37.030
have a sliding frame of data.

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:40.770
So for example, if you look
at the current row of data,

00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:45.780
you can look at the
last five preceding rows

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:50.250
and write something like
a moving average, which

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:54.840
is obviously a very important
analytical calculation.

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:57.600
So this has actually
been in this SQL standard

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.220
for many years.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:01.920
And Postgres has
had support in that

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:05.340
for coming up for 10 years now.

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:06.990
But in the latest
release we've got

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:10.650
these fine-grained
extensions, really,

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:15.180
that put the finishing
touches to Postgres' support.

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:17.400
And the ones that you
mentioned there really

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:20.610
are just important in
really just getting

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:23.280
the last few dregs
of information

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:25.500
out of that style of query.

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:27.210
And what's really
surprising, though,

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:30.450
is we are finishing
the implementation

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:31.530
of these features.

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:33.870
And yet many people have
never even heard of them.

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:34.770
OK?

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:38.820
And so sometimes when
we talk about SQL

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:42.510
a kind of boring language that
existed from 30 years ago,

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:45.300
people are not really
moving with the fact

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:49.880
that it's actually evolved
very considerably over time.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:50.630
DAN MCCLARY: Yeah.

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:52.213
Modern SQL is really something.

00:24:52.213 --> 00:24:53.630
To think a little
bit about that--

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:56.030
to the finer grains
and a frame exclusion,

00:24:56.030 --> 00:24:57.390
I might have my moving average.

00:24:57.390 --> 00:25:00.800
But I might want to say, well,
give me the last five values,

00:25:00.800 --> 00:25:01.980
but remove the duplicates.

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:02.480
Right?

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:04.940
And that could actually have
a really material change

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:06.260
on the average in computing.

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:08.930
And again, that sliding
window doesn't necessarily

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:10.590
have to be rows
proceeding anymore.

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:11.090
Right?

00:25:11.090 --> 00:25:12.140
It can now be groups.

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:14.600
And so if I'm actually talking
about group by averages,

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.850
that can be really interesting.

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:17.350
All right.

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:19.630
So let's talk a little
bit about data types.

00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:23.470
I think Postgres already has got
a really rich support for data

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:25.660
types, whether it be
array types, structure

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:27.665
types, domains.

00:25:27.665 --> 00:25:29.290
But I think one area
that's interesting

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:33.700
is semi-structured data, because
JSON and JSON/B have become,

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:38.230
I think, interesting additions
to Postgres in general.

00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:41.598
And in particular, I think at
11 we got full text indexing.

00:25:41.598 --> 00:25:42.640
Or it might have been 10.

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:44.930
I'm not 100% certain.

00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:47.980
So are you seeing more use
of semi-structured data

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:51.767
in the Postgres world now that
these features are available?

00:25:51.767 --> 00:25:52.850
SIMON RIGGS: Very much so.

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:56.690
I think people have
bought into the concepts

00:25:56.690 --> 00:25:59.870
of semi-structured data from
a lot of other databases.

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:02.720
But they want to
use those concepts

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:05.210
within a normal
database structure.

00:26:05.210 --> 00:26:09.500
So they want to be able to
mix the concepts together.

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:12.590
Relational works,
but up to a point.

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:14.960
And then when once
you go sort of past

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:18.110
that point where you
hit the complexity wall,

00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:20.510
semi-structured data
is really useful.

00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:23.420
So it's great to be able
to just have a column

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:27.000
where you have sort
of data blobs in.

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:30.600
And JSON and JSON/B is
just excellent for that.

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:32.690
So we have lots of
people using it.

00:26:32.690 --> 00:26:36.500
So it's not something
that's in the back rooms.

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:40.080
It's really one of our
key features these days.

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:44.180
The JSON/B data type
compresses the data.

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:46.370
So we store it
compressed, but in a way

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:49.700
that you can still search on
the compressed data, which

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:52.530
obviously is a very
important feature.

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:57.260
So the full text support that
we've released in Postgres 11

00:26:57.260 --> 00:27:00.440
is actually enabling
two things that

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:02.210
were in Postgres previously.

00:27:02.210 --> 00:27:04.710
Obviously, the JSON data type.

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:07.310
But the full text
support allowed

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:12.600
us to build indexes on data,
but in a very complex way.

00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:16.820
So we could have things
like a Russian grammar

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:20.510
for words in the JSON blobs.

00:27:20.510 --> 00:27:25.010
And you've got things like
a stemmer that will take

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:27.210
the front portion of the word.

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:32.030
So instead of a word
that ends with -ing,

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:38.232
we can just take off the -ing,
and it'll be the main word

00:27:38.232 --> 00:27:39.440
that we're talking about now.

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:42.140
And we can just
index that instead.

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:44.810
So that the full
support of full text

00:27:44.810 --> 00:27:48.770
is now available on
top of JSON/B data.

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:52.385
So a very important combination.

00:27:52.385 --> 00:27:54.010
DAN MCCLARY: That's
really interesting.

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:55.750
I think one of the things
that that kind of highlights

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:57.667
between thinking about
the analytic functions,

00:27:57.667 --> 00:27:59.590
thinking about
semi-structured data types,

00:27:59.590 --> 00:28:01.480
and being able to
actually effectively do

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:05.520
complex processing on them, is
that I often think of Postgres

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:07.340
as it's an OLTP system mostly.

00:28:07.340 --> 00:28:10.660
But the reality of it is it's
really a multi-model database

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:11.720
in a lot of ways.

00:28:11.720 --> 00:28:14.195
Like, if you need to do things
that are somewhat key value

00:28:14.195 --> 00:28:15.820
oriented, if you need
to do things that

00:28:15.820 --> 00:28:19.930
are more sort of Elasticsearch
full text indexing, like,

00:28:19.930 --> 00:28:21.772
these are all capabilities
available to you.

00:28:21.772 --> 00:28:24.230
And if you need to bring them
all together in one database,

00:28:24.230 --> 00:28:25.605
Postgres is really
about the best

00:28:25.605 --> 00:28:27.967
you can do in the
open source world.

00:28:27.967 --> 00:28:29.050
SIMON RIGGS: Very much so.

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:31.383
I mean, I think that's really
been the power of Postgres

00:28:31.383 --> 00:28:37.130
is while many vendors
have been highlighting,

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:40.070
I've got this great database
as long as you accept

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:42.150
this long list of restrictions.

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:44.410
OK?

00:28:44.410 --> 00:28:46.560
And then there's
another database server

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:49.130
that has a different long
list of restrictions.

00:28:49.130 --> 00:28:50.870
And trying to build
an application that

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:55.040
integrates different
servers together

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:56.870
works at the prototype stage.

00:28:56.870 --> 00:29:00.260
And then someone says,
how will I back it up?

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:03.630
And simple things like
that become really complex.

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:05.570
And what Postgres
has done is said,

00:29:05.570 --> 00:29:07.850
really there aren't
any restrictions.

00:29:07.850 --> 00:29:10.700
You can have all
manner of data types

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:12.890
with all manner of index types.

00:29:12.890 --> 00:29:17.960
And we support OLTP queries,
longer analytical queries.

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:20.360
We support graph
queries, for example.

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:21.590
Not very well-known.

00:29:21.590 --> 00:29:22.980
Window queries.

00:29:22.980 --> 00:29:27.440
And this really gives
you a breadth to Postgres

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:31.040
that means that when you're
building an application,

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.320
you can be pretty
certain that you will be

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:35.780
able to build it on Postgres.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:37.880
Whereas some of the
other platforms,

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:40.790
you have to look carefully
at the list of restrictions

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:43.410
before you decide
whether to use it or not.

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:48.500
And if your restrictions change
after your initial build,

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:50.240
you're in problems.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:52.070
And that's really the
strength of Postgres

00:29:52.070 --> 00:29:57.350
has been the confidence that
it gives application builders.

00:29:57.350 --> 00:30:00.000
Because as I said,
you can be certain

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:01.990
that Postgres will run it.

00:30:01.990 --> 00:30:02.740
DAN MCCLARY: Yeah.

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:05.170
It's actually a great platform
to avoid pre-optimization.

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:05.670
Right?

00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:07.160
You can optimize
when you need to.

00:30:07.160 --> 00:30:10.390
But if you're not quite sure,
Postgres will probably at least

00:30:10.390 --> 00:30:12.820
get you most of the way.

00:30:12.820 --> 00:30:13.320
All right.

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:14.430
So it's a new release.

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:17.055
And you can't have a new release
of a database without somebody

00:30:17.055 --> 00:30:19.350
want to talk about
speeds and feeds.

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:22.065
So this is not any kind
of an official benchmark.

00:30:22.065 --> 00:30:23.940
This is just I spent a
little bit of time one

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:26.130
afternoon looking
at Postgres 9.6

00:30:26.130 --> 00:30:29.435
on Cloud SQL and
Postgres 11 on cloud SQL.

00:30:29.435 --> 00:30:32.910
And I was just looking
at effectively tpm-C.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.710
And I think I see some
interesting things.

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:36.210
Right?

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:38.850
The theoretical
throughput is up overall.

00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:41.283
We see improved behaviors.

00:30:41.283 --> 00:30:43.200
But I know there's been
a bunch of work that's

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:46.800
gone into the planner, into
the stats collection, things

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:48.210
like that.

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:50.940
Anything you want to say broadly
about overall performance

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:54.210
in 11, or things that users
should be aware of, they should

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:56.610
be taking advantage
of, that maybe they

00:30:56.610 --> 00:30:59.520
didn't read deeply enough in
the liner notes to know about?

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:01.770
SIMON RIGGS: Well, thank you
for asking that question,

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:04.830
because the things
I like to highlight

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:08.250
is those things that are
kind of buried in the notes.

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:12.150
But really I'd like to celebrate
what you see on the screen

00:31:12.150 --> 00:31:14.010
there, because if you look--

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:16.690
I mean, the numbers
there are extraordinary.

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:21.570
We've gone from
8,000 tpm to 12,500,

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:27.160
which is more than a 50%
gain in performance in OLTP.

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:28.920
And that's really
just one workload

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:31.370
that has been optimized,
because there's

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:34.120
a whole range of other
workloads that we support.

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:37.918
So I mean, that in itself is--
we could talk for five minutes,

00:31:37.918 --> 00:31:38.710
really, about that.

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:44.310
But really to answer your wider
question, what we've been doing

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:50.130
is not just expanding the
execution speed of queries

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:54.000
with things like aggregation
improvements or parallel query,

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:57.900
but we've also looked at
the statistical information

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:02.170
that we keep within
the database.

00:32:02.170 --> 00:32:05.460
So we now support something
called the create statistics

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:06.350
command.

00:32:06.350 --> 00:32:12.300
And what that allows you
to do is tell the database

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:15.390
that multiple columns
are actually linked.

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:21.150
For example, there's a
common example of in the US

00:32:21.150 --> 00:32:23.740
you have area code and state.

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:27.870
Now we all know the area
code and state are related,

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:29.880
but the optimizer doesn't.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:33.270
And it's only when you
tell it that these two

00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:35.470
columns are actually related.

00:32:35.470 --> 00:32:42.540
And the area code for Los
Angeles is not the area--

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:45.030
it's not possible
to have a customer

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:48.930
with an area code in Los Angeles
when they live in New Jersey.

00:32:48.930 --> 00:32:50.130
It just doesn't happen.

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:51.190
OK?

00:32:51.190 --> 00:32:54.930
And so the optimizer can use
those types of information

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.840
to really change the
selectivity estimates.

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:00.750
And when you get the
selectivity estimates right,

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:02.970
you get the right query plan.

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:07.260
And most of the reason for
poor performing queries

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:11.220
is actually because of
the optimizer's ability

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:13.570
to understand what's happening.

00:33:13.570 --> 00:33:17.430
So I don't want to denigrate
the massive work done

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:19.500
on parallel query at all.

00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:24.030
But we've done an equal amount
of work on the optimizer,

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:25.660
on statistics.

00:33:25.660 --> 00:33:29.430
So for example,
in Postgres 10 we

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:35.710
introduced for the first time
foreign keys into planning.

00:33:35.710 --> 00:33:39.730
So previously, foreign keys
were just a constraint.

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:40.230
Right?

00:33:40.230 --> 00:33:43.020
Whereas now the foreign
keys are actually taken into

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:44.760
account in the planning.

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:47.610
And that means you get much
better plans and faster queries

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:48.730
as a result.

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:53.320
So those are not
really admin tasks.

00:33:53.320 --> 00:33:56.270
There's some pretty cool admin
things out there as well.

00:33:56.270 --> 00:34:01.480
But really performance across
all of the different workloads

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:04.913
has improved really
quite considerably.

00:34:04.913 --> 00:34:06.080
DAN MCCLARY: That's awesome.

00:34:06.080 --> 00:34:06.580
Yeah.

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:09.931
I think that the notion of
understanding that we can now

00:34:09.931 --> 00:34:12.139
actually help improve those
plans in ways we couldn't

00:34:12.139 --> 00:34:15.469
before, any of us we've
ever run a benchmark

00:34:15.469 --> 00:34:18.770
know that if you don't
get a good plan--

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:20.060
it's just like life.

00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:22.440
It's just like life.

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:22.940
All right.

00:34:22.940 --> 00:34:25.610
Well, Simon, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:34:25.610 --> 00:34:27.580
Everybody, give it
up for Simon Riggs.

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:30.679
CEO of 2ndQuadrant.

00:34:30.679 --> 00:34:33.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:33.300 --> 00:34:33.800
Wow.

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:36.530
There's a lot of stuff PG 11 you
guys have got to go check out.

00:34:36.530 --> 00:34:38.699
It's really, really cool.

00:34:38.699 --> 00:34:40.699
So let's talk just briefly
about the year ahead.

00:34:40.699 --> 00:34:42.409
And then I'll hand
it back over to Ori,

00:34:42.409 --> 00:34:44.900
who's going to give us
another taste of SQL Server,

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:47.679
in case you didn't see
it on the keynote stage.

00:34:47.679 --> 00:34:49.767
So in 2019 there's
a lot of stuff

00:34:49.767 --> 00:34:51.600
we're interested in--
new database versions,

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:54.477
new databases, for example.

00:34:54.477 --> 00:34:57.060
Some security features we think
are really important, customer

00:34:57.060 --> 00:34:59.190
managing encryption keys.

00:34:59.190 --> 00:35:01.470
This is a broad requirement
for all of Google Cloud.

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:04.380
And you're going to see it
in Cloud SQL very, very soon.

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:05.340
Richer auditing.

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:08.520
Simon and I talked a little
bit about regulated industry,

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:10.110
financial services, health care.

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:12.930
We really want to improve
the level of auditability

00:35:12.930 --> 00:35:15.390
in Cloud SQL so
that we're yet again

00:35:15.390 --> 00:35:18.355
further ready for these
kinds of workloads.

00:35:18.355 --> 00:35:19.980
We're also going to
spend a lot of time

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:23.220
this year focusing
on manageability.

00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:25.710
If you've run Cloud SQL and
you wonder about maintenance

00:35:25.710 --> 00:35:27.660
events, we know.

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:28.980
We know.

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:31.290
We're working on both
deferrals and event controls.

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:32.665
And these are
things we're really

00:35:32.665 --> 00:35:37.590
going to focus on making at
least first class in the coming

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:38.700
year.

00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:40.770
Point-in-Time recovery
for all of our databases

00:35:40.770 --> 00:35:42.040
is a huge priority.

00:35:42.040 --> 00:35:43.710
We already have it
in the MySQL world.

00:35:43.710 --> 00:35:45.150
You'll see it in Postgres.

00:35:45.150 --> 00:35:46.983
And obviously, you'll
see it in SQL servers.

00:35:46.983 --> 00:35:50.120
And that's something you expect.

00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:51.750
Our overall
manageability UI we're

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:54.550
going to invest a lot of time
into and try and improve that.

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:58.080
And we're also going to start to
begin a journey towards greater

00:35:58.080 --> 00:36:00.210
federation with the rest
of the GCP platform.

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:02.730
One of the areas that
we expect to invest in--

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:05.275
this year's federation
from BigQuery,

00:36:05.275 --> 00:36:06.900
which is to say if
you have a warehouse

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:08.460
stored in Google
BigQuery, you'll

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:10.560
be able to reach
into your Postgres

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:16.075
database and query operational
data that may live there.

00:36:16.075 --> 00:36:18.450
We're also very interested in
potentially having Postgres

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:19.800
reach out to the rest of GCP.

00:36:19.800 --> 00:36:21.690
We think that's a
really interesting area

00:36:21.690 --> 00:36:23.922
that we're going to
investigate going forward.

00:36:23.922 --> 00:36:26.130
And with that, I'm going to
go ahead and turn it over

00:36:26.130 --> 00:36:28.130
to Ori, who's going to
tell us about SQL Server.

00:36:28.130 --> 00:36:29.010
ORI KASHI: All right.

00:36:29.010 --> 00:36:31.600
Thank you very much.

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:32.170
Hi, everyone.

00:36:32.170 --> 00:36:34.950
So I am here today to talk
to you about a product

00:36:34.950 --> 00:36:36.390
you I'm sure have
never heard of--

00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:38.280
SQL Server.

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:41.280
We're really excited to
announce that we've expanded

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:43.020
our portfolio for Cloud SQL.

00:36:43.020 --> 00:36:45.880
And now we will be offering
managed SQL Server.

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:48.900
Currently we're launching
our closed alpha.

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:52.080
Beta will come towards
the end of this year.

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:54.330
And that will be open
to all customers.

00:36:54.330 --> 00:36:57.030
Now a lot of what Dan
was talking about,

00:36:57.030 --> 00:37:01.020
and why it's so important to
be part of the Cloud SQL family

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:03.120
is really that SQL
Server is going

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:06.300
to take advantage of a lot
of the platform goodness

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:08.700
that we're bringing already
to MySQL and Postgres

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:13.800
over the coming year,
and leverage the same set

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:14.320
of features.

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:15.360
So when you talk about--

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:17.100
I get the question all the time.

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:19.410
What regions is SQL Server
going to be available in?

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:21.930
The answer is, everywhere
that Cloud SQL is available,

00:37:21.930 --> 00:37:24.300
that's where SQL
Server is available.

00:37:24.300 --> 00:37:27.540
And the same goes for what are
automated backup stories going

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:28.040
to be.

00:37:28.040 --> 00:37:29.738
Point-in-Time
recovery is coming.

00:37:29.738 --> 00:37:30.780
Patching story, all that.

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:33.690
We are going to have kind
of this unified experience

00:37:33.690 --> 00:37:36.840
that you can come to expect
as a standard from GCP

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:38.730
for admin and services.

00:37:38.730 --> 00:37:40.480
And SQL Server will
be no exception.

00:37:40.480 --> 00:37:43.200
And so that's going to add
hopefully a lot of value,

00:37:43.200 --> 00:37:47.430
reduce a lot of the overhead
in terms of managing SQL Server

00:37:47.430 --> 00:37:49.800
and how it interacts
with your apps.

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:52.660
Additionally, I want to talk a
little bit about compatibility.

00:37:52.660 --> 00:37:56.040
So SQL Server-- in
most organizations

00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:58.860
that we've run through with
a lot of the customers,

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:02.368
they already have kind of
their workflows baked in.

00:38:02.368 --> 00:38:03.660
They have their favorite tools.

00:38:03.660 --> 00:38:06.900
They have their third
party analytics services

00:38:06.900 --> 00:38:09.780
or whatever else they're kind
of running on top of SQL Server.

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:12.570
One of our top goals is
actually to make sure

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:16.140
that we are able to preserve
that level of compatibility.

00:38:16.140 --> 00:38:18.120
So we're not going to
introduce something

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:19.950
where you have to
break your normal flow,

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:22.505
retrain a bunch of your folks.

00:38:22.505 --> 00:38:24.630
We expect you to be able
to continue doing business

00:38:24.630 --> 00:38:27.655
as usual, just with a managed
service in the background

00:38:27.655 --> 00:38:30.030
to reduce, again, a lot of
that maintenance and overhead.

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:35.520
Just some quick bullets.

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:39.330
So SQL Server 2017 is
going to be the version

00:38:39.330 --> 00:38:41.070
that we're going out with.

00:38:41.070 --> 00:38:44.610
As I said, there is kind of this
versioning strategy across all

00:38:44.610 --> 00:38:47.280
of our managed database
services that we will continue

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:49.890
to keep up-to-date with, of
course, the minor revisions.

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:51.750
And as the major
versions come out too,

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:55.350
we'll evaluate them and
make them available as well.

00:38:55.350 --> 00:38:58.020
We're starting out with
our Alpha Standard Edition.

00:38:58.020 --> 00:39:00.390
But we will at the
time of Beta and GA

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:03.540
have all editions
available to our customers.

00:39:03.540 --> 00:39:05.328
The VM shapes that
are underlying

00:39:05.328 --> 00:39:07.620
this are going to be the same
as the rest of Cloud SQL.

00:39:07.620 --> 00:39:10.380
Again, there's that
kind of standardization

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:12.060
across the Cloud SQL family.

00:39:12.060 --> 00:39:15.030
And so everything that you see
that customized I think already

00:39:15.030 --> 00:39:17.538
in Postgres, there's
custom VM shapes,

00:39:17.538 --> 00:39:19.830
that's actually the standard
that we're going to adopt.

00:39:19.830 --> 00:39:21.060
The only limitation,
of course, is

00:39:21.060 --> 00:39:23.430
going to be the different
editions of SQL Server have

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:25.800
kind of baked-in
limits on what they

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:30.040
can utilize as far as memory
and cores and things like that.

00:39:30.040 --> 00:39:33.640
And then finally, again, Dan
touched on this a little bit

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:36.170
with the comment about
federation with BigQuery.

00:39:36.170 --> 00:39:39.240
But I think it bears
repeating that integration

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:40.860
with Stackdriver,
integration with all

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:44.130
the other available
tools and services

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:47.065
that kind of proliferate
around the GCP ecosystem

00:39:47.065 --> 00:39:48.690
is going to be one
of the biggest value

00:39:48.690 --> 00:39:49.440
adds that we have.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:52.950
And SQL Server is going to
benefit a lot from that.

00:39:52.950 --> 00:39:56.520
And so if you are already
kind of a customer for any

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:59.640
of our other services-- using
Compute, using something else--

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:02.910
this should be an easy add-on
if you have a stubborn workload

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:06.300
that's remained on prem,
or in a VM, even in GCE

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:09.030
that you want to kind of
go to a managed model.

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:11.340
That's something that
will be available to you.

00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:13.465
I think the slides are
available after the session.

00:40:13.465 --> 00:40:15.660
So there is a link, if you
are interested in Alpha.

00:40:15.660 --> 00:40:17.850
To show your interest, sign up.

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:20.340
We have a set of
customers selected now.

00:40:20.340 --> 00:40:23.280
But we'll be expanding the
Alpha as we get closer to Beta.

00:40:23.280 --> 00:40:25.020
And we welcome any
interested parties.

00:40:25.020 --> 00:40:29.210
We're happy to have
you if there's space.

00:40:29.210 --> 00:40:33.400
And with that, everyone's
favorite thing-- a demo.

00:40:33.400 --> 00:40:37.120
So this is actually running
on SQL Server 2008 R2.

00:40:37.120 --> 00:40:39.610
It's going end-of-life,
obviously, later on this year.

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:41.350
In just a few short
months actually.

00:40:41.350 --> 00:40:44.840
Team that we start out with--
.bak as the first supported

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:46.420
back and restore file type.

00:40:46.420 --> 00:40:48.800
Obviously, that's the
native one for SQL Server.

00:40:48.800 --> 00:40:51.467
I know the team worked very hard
to make sure that that is first

00:40:51.467 --> 00:40:52.510
out the gate.

00:40:52.510 --> 00:40:55.360
And then after that we actually
have an incredibly simple

00:40:55.360 --> 00:40:57.030
import flow.

00:40:57.030 --> 00:41:00.450
And it's very similar to what
you've already seen for .SQL

00:41:00.450 --> 00:41:02.340
and .CSV imports.

00:41:02.340 --> 00:41:08.160
But I'm just going to go and
select this .bak file that

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:11.412
I've uploaded previously.

00:41:11.412 --> 00:41:13.680
There we go.

00:41:13.680 --> 00:41:15.370
I'll just name it too.

00:41:15.370 --> 00:41:21.705
Let's call this skidb001.

00:41:21.705 --> 00:41:24.120
I'll click Import.

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:27.950
And while that is
happening, I'll actually--

00:41:27.950 --> 00:41:30.250
this usually only
takes a minute--

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:31.380
I'll copy over the IP.

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:35.310
I've got the configuration
file over here.

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:36.085
I'll paste it.

00:41:40.625 --> 00:41:50.554
And with any luck it
should still be up.

00:41:50.554 --> 00:41:52.940
Yup.

00:41:52.940 --> 00:41:54.120
All set.

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:57.900
So now this web app is running
on our managed service.

00:41:57.900 --> 00:42:00.440
And it was that simple.

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:03.580
And that's actually our
goal for our customers.

00:42:03.580 --> 00:42:07.030
So we want to make sure that
if you have more complex apps,

00:42:07.030 --> 00:42:09.280
obviously, it's going to be
more than this [INAUDIBLE]

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:11.490
process I just showed you.

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:15.503
But we want to provide you
with the tooling, not just

00:42:15.503 --> 00:42:17.420
the simple flows, but
also through third party

00:42:17.420 --> 00:42:19.040
relationships,
through other tool

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:20.332
sets that we'll make available.

00:42:20.332 --> 00:42:22.707
You, of course, heard about
the Illumina acquisition that

00:42:22.707 --> 00:42:24.170
happened earlier this year--

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:26.090
or just earlier this month.

00:42:26.090 --> 00:42:28.880
And we're really doubling
down on making sure

00:42:28.880 --> 00:42:31.970
that the migration onto GCP,
onto our managed services,

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:34.370
is smooth and painless.

00:42:34.370 --> 00:42:38.960
One other thing I wanted to call
out before we get to the Q&amp;A

00:42:38.960 --> 00:42:40.890
is actually two things.

00:42:40.890 --> 00:42:44.120
One is a quick shout out to
our Managed AD service that

00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:46.490
